    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d733">

                <pb facs="0793" n="689" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg689"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5041: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland (Wilson) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-04-13T16:00:00+01:00">April 13, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 13—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>80. Yen told me this morning that he had requested a meeting of the 19 which will
                    be held on Saturday<anchor n="59" xml:id="d733-f1"/> in order to present the
                    Chinese point of view on the Shanghai negotiations. He said that China could not
                    accept the proposal of Sir Miles Lampson even in its modified form as it
                    contained “conditions” for withdrawal of the Japanese forces and thus was
                    contrary to the Assembly resolution. For example, withdrawal was not to take
                    effect for 6 months and then only if order in Shanghai and the surrounding
                    district justified such withdrawal.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
